<p class="lead">A landscape lighting system is only as good as the way it turns on and off. Lights that come on at the wrong time, stay on all night, or need a trip to the garage to adjust get ignored or switched off for good. Spring Landscape Lighting installs smart landscape lighting controls that let the system run itself: astronomical timers that follow sunset through the year, separate zones for different parts of the property, dimming, and app control when you want it. You get the right light at the right hour without thinking about it.</p>

<h2>What's included</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Evaluation of your existing transformer and controls, or specification of new ones for a new system.</li>
  <li>Astronomical timers or photocells so lights switch on at dusk without seasonal resetting.</li>
  <li>Zoning: separating facade, trees, paths and patio so each can run its own schedule.</li>
  <li>Dimming at the zone or fixture level where the equipment supports it.</li>
  <li>Wi-Fi or app-based control, scene setup and a walkthrough so you know how to use it.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Control options compared</h2>
<div class="grid-cards">
  <div class="mini-card"><h3>Mechanical timers</h3><p>Simple and inexpensive, but they drift with daylight saving and the seasons, and power outages reset them.</p></div>
  <div class="mini-card"><h3>Photocells</h3><p>Turn lights on at dusk automatically. Often paired with a timer for the off time, and must be placed where they will not see the system's own light.</p></div>
  <div class="mini-card"><h3>Astronomical timers</h3><p>Calculate sunrise and sunset for your location every day. Set once, they follow the calendar through the whole year.</p></div>
  <div class="mini-card"><h3>App-based systems</h3><p>Smart transformers or Wi-Fi modules that offer zones, scenes, dimming and remote control from a phone.</p></div>
</div>
<p>Sunset in Spring, TX moves by roughly two hours across the year, from around 5:20 p.m. in early December to after 8:20 p.m. in late June. A fixed-time mechanical timer is either late in winter or early in summer. Our <a href="/tools/sunset-timer-planner/">sunset and timer planner</a> shows month-by-month sunset times for Spring and suggests schedules, along with how many hours a year your system will actually run.</p>

<div class="table-wrap">
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Zone</th><th>Typical schedule</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Facade and front trees</td><td>Dusk to 11 p.m. or midnight</td><td>Curb appeal while neighbors and guests are around</td></tr>
<tr><td>Paths and driveway</td><td>Dusk to bedtime, or all night at low level</td><td>Safe arrival, late returns</td></tr>
<tr><td>Patio and pool</td><td>Manual or scene-based</td><td>Only needed when the space is in use</td></tr>
<tr><td>Security-oriented zones</td><td>Dusk to dawn at reduced brightness</td><td>Visibility without glare all night</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>

<h2>Considerations for Spring, TX homes</h2>
<h3>Storms and power outages</h3>
<p>Thunderstorms and hurricane season bring outages. Mechanical timers lose their setting when power drops, so lights may come on at noon for weeks before anyone notices. Astronomical and app-based controllers typically hold their schedule, and we confirm how each one behaves after an outage before recommending it.</p>
<h3>Wi-Fi reach</h3>
<p>Transformers usually live on an exterior wall, often on the side of the garage, where home Wi-Fi can be weak. We check signal strength at the mounting location and suggest an extender or a hub-based system if needed.</p>
<h3>Heat and enclosures</h3>
<p>Control equipment in an outdoor enclosure on a west-facing wall can get very hot in August. We mount transformers and controllers out of direct afternoon sun where practical and use weather-rated enclosures.</p>
<h3>Neighbors and HOAs</h3>
<p>Some neighborhoods and many neighbors appreciate lights that dim or switch off at a reasonable hour. Scheduling the facade to turn off at 11 p.m. while path lights stay at a low level is a considerate default.</p>

<h2>Common mistakes we avoid</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Everything on one circuit.</strong> Without zones, you cannot turn off the facade without losing the path lights.</li>
  <li><strong>Photocells that see the lights.</strong> A photocell aimed at a lit wall can cycle on and off all night. Placement matters.</li>
  <li><strong>Incompatible dimming.</strong> Not every LED fixture dims smoothly on every controller. We match equipment so dimming is flicker-free.</li>
  <li><strong>Dusk-to-dawn on everything.</strong> It wastes energy, adds light pollution and shortens fixture life with no real benefit.</li>
  <li><strong>Overcomplicated apps.</strong> A system that needs a manual to turn the patio lights on is a system nobody uses. We set clear scenes and names.</li>
</ul>

<div class="callout"><strong>Tip:</strong> If your lights are on during the day, the problem is almost always a timer that lost its setting or a faulty photocell. See our <a href="/services/landscape-lighting-repair/">landscape lighting repair</a> page for other common symptoms.</div>

<h2>Upgrading an existing system</h2>
<p>Controls are one of the easiest upgrades to make to an older installation. Many systems can gain an astronomical timer or a smart module without replacing fixtures. If you are also converting from halogen, it is a good moment to rethink zones, and our article on <a href="/halogen-to-led-landscape-lighting/">converting halogen landscape lighting to LED</a> covers what else to check. Smart controls work with every part of a design, from <a href="/services/architectural-lighting/">architectural lighting</a> to <a href="/services/patio-pool-lighting/">patio and pool lighting</a>. Spring Landscape Lighting can review your current setup and tell you what is worth changing.</p>

